window.COURSE = {"sections":[{"id":"overview","label":"Start Here","title":"🕰️ Abigail's Past Tense Course","group":"Start","groupEmoji":"🏁","emoji":"🕰️","blurb":"The five units, the photo-album idea, and your 25-minute plan.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p><strong>Hello Abigail!</strong> 👋</p>\n<p>Some things already happened. They are in <strong>the past</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Yesterday I <strong>was</strong> at the beach.</em> 🏖️</li>\n<li><em>I <strong>played</strong> football.</em> ⚽</li>\n<li><em>I <strong>didn't</strong> finish my homework.</em> 📚</li>\n<li><em>I <strong>was running</strong> when I fell.</em> 🏃</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course teaches you <strong>all five ways</strong> to talk about the past. It follows your\ngrammar book, <strong>Units 10 to 14</strong>, and it takes <strong>25 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/hero.jpg\" alt=\"A girl holding open a photo album with small glowing pictures of yesterday, a birthday and a beach floating out\">\n<figcaption>The past is just yesterday in a photo album 📸</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>⏱️ Your 25 minutes</h2>\n<div id=\"paceTimer\"></div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🗺️ The five units</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>What you learn</th>\n<th>Say it like this</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>10</strong></td>\n<td><strong>was / were</strong></td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was</strong> tired. They <strong>were</strong> happy.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>11</strong></td>\n<td><strong>worked / got / went</strong> (past simple)</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>played</strong>. She <strong>went</strong> home.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>12</strong></td>\n<td><strong>I didn't … Did you … ?</strong> ⭐</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>didn't go</strong>. <strong>Did you</strong> go?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>13</strong></td>\n<td><strong>I was doing</strong> (past continuous)</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was reading</strong> at 8 o'clock.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>14</strong></td>\n<td><strong>I was doing</strong> and <strong>I did</strong> together</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was walking</strong> when it <strong>started</strong> to rain.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>⭐ <strong>Unit 12 gets extra attention in this course</strong> — it has its own big step, its\nown cheat-sheet page, and the longest quiz. That's because nearly everybody makes\nthe same mistake there. You won't. 😉</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The big picture (read this once)</h2>\n<p>Think of the past like a <strong>photo album</strong> 📸:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>was/were</strong> = describing the photo (<em>\"The sea <strong>was</strong> blue.\"</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>past simple</strong> = one photo, one finished action (<em>\"We <strong>swam</strong>.\"</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>didn't / Did you…?</strong> = the same photo, but <strong>saying no</strong> or <strong>asking</strong>\n  (<em>\"We <strong>didn't swim</strong>.\" / \"<strong>Did</strong> you swim?\"</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>was doing</strong> = a video clip, still playing (<em>\"We <strong>were swimming</strong> at 3 o'clock.\"</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>was doing + did</strong> = a video with a surprise in the middle (<em>\"We <strong>were\n  swimming</strong> when a wave <strong>knocked</strong> me over.\"</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>One album, five ways to talk about it.</strong> 📖</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⭐ How to get stars</h2>\n<p>Each step has a quiz. Answer them and collect stars. Get them all and you are a\n<strong>Past Tense Champion</strong>! 🏆</p>\n<p>👇 <strong>Start with Unit 10: was / were.</strong></p>"},{"id":"dashboard","label":"My Stars","title":"My Stars","group":"Start","groupEmoji":"🏁","emoji":"📈","blurb":"Your stars and quiz scores - saved in this browser.","kind":"dashboard","quiz":null,"html":""},{"id":"unit-10","label":"Unit 10: was / were","title":"🏖️ Unit 10: was / were","group":"The Past Tense","groupEmoji":"•","emoji":"🏖️","blurb":"The past of am / is / are - and how to say no and ask.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Unit 10 — was / were","questions":[{"q":"Yesterday I ______ at the beach.","options":["was","were","am"],"answer":0,"why":"'I' takes WAS in the past. (I was / he was / she was / it was)"},{"q":"They ______ very tired after the match.","options":["was","were","is"],"answer":1,"why":"'They' (more than one) takes WERE."},{"q":"She ______ at school last Monday.","options":["were","was","are"],"answer":1,"why":"'She' is one person → WAS."},{"q":"Which is the correct negative?","options":["I weren't happy.","I wasn't happy.","I didn't was happy."],"answer":1,"why":"'I' → WASN'T. And was/were never use 'did'!"},{"q":"Make it a question: 'You were at home.'","options":["Were you at home?","You were at home?","Did you were at home?"],"answer":0,"why":"Just move WERE to the front. No 'did' needed."},{"q":"'Were you at school?' — correct short answer?","options":["Yes.","Yes, I was.","Yes, I did."],"answer":1,"why":"Short answers always repeat the helping word: 'Yes, I was.' Never answer with just 'Yes'."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 0:00 → 5:00 (5 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Learn the <strong>past of <em>am / is / are</em></strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The rule (very short)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Now (present)</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>am</strong></td>\n<td>I <strong>was</strong></td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was</strong> at the beach yesterday.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>he / she / it <strong>is</strong></td>\n<td>he / she / it <strong>was</strong></td>\n<td><em>She <strong>was</strong> happy.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you / we / they <strong>are</strong></td>\n<td>you / we / they <strong>were</strong></td>\n<td><em>They <strong>were</strong> tired.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🎵 Say this rhythm:</h2>\n<h2>I was · he was · she was · it was</h2>\n<h2>you were · we were · they were</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>💡 <strong>Trick:</strong> <em>was</em> has just <strong>one</strong> letter change from <em>is</em> — and it's the\n<strong>singular</strong> one. <em>Were</em> rhymes with <em>there</em> and goes with <strong>more than one</strong> (and\nwith <em>you</em>, even when <em>you</em> is one person! 😄).</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/waswere.jpg\" alt=\"A girl and a boy on a beach at sunset with sandcastles and a beach ball\">\n<figcaption>*\"The beach **was** beautiful. We **were** very happy.\"* 🏖️</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Describing yesterday with was/were</h2>\n<p>Use <em>was/were</em> to <strong>describe</strong> — the weather, feelings, places, people:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Yesterday it <strong>was</strong> sunny.</em> ☀️</li>\n<li><em>The water <strong>was</strong> cold!</em> 🌊</li>\n<li><em>I <strong>was</strong> hungry after swimming.</em> 🍜</li>\n<li><em>My friends <strong>were</strong> at the beach too.</em> 👫</li>\n<li><em>They <strong>were</strong> very good at volleyball.</em> 🏐</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Saying NO: wasn't / weren't</h2>\n<p>Just add <strong>not</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Positive</th>\n<th>❌ Negative (short)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I was</td>\n<td>I <strong>wasn't</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She was</td>\n<td>She <strong>wasn't</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We were</td>\n<td>We <strong>weren't</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They were</td>\n<td>They <strong>weren't</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I <strong>wasn't</strong> tired.</em></li>\n<li><em>There <strong>weren't</strong> any fish.</em> 🐟</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Asking: Was …? Were …?</h2>\n<p><strong>Move the word to the front</strong> — that's all:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Telling</th>\n<th>Asking</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>You <strong>were</strong> at school.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Were</strong> you at school?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>was</strong> late.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Was</strong> she late?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Short answers</strong> (never say just \"yes\" or \"no\"!):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Yes</th>\n<th>No</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Were you at school?</em></td>\n<td><em>Yes, I <strong>was</strong>.</em></td>\n<td><em>No, I <strong>wasn't</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Was she late?</em></td>\n<td><em>Yes, she <strong>was</strong>.</em></td>\n<td><em>No, she <strong>wasn't</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ The mistake to avoid</h2>\n<p>❌ <em>I <strong>were</strong> happy.</em> → ✅ <em>I <strong>was</strong> happy.</em></p>\n<p>❌ <em>They <strong>was</strong> at the park.</em> → ✅ <em>They <strong>were</strong> at the park.</em></p>\n<p><strong>One person (I, he, she, it) = was. More than one (we, they) = were.</strong>\nAnd remember: <strong><em>you</em> always takes <em>were</em></strong> — even for one person. 🙂</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 was/were + places</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I <strong>was</strong> at home.</em> 🏠</li>\n<li><em>She <strong>was</strong> in the garden.</em> 🌷</li>\n<li><em>We <strong>were</strong> on the bus.</em> 🚌</li>\n<li><em>They <strong>were</strong> at the cinema.</em> 🎬</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Careful!</strong> <em>I was <strong>at</strong> home</em> (not \"in home\"). But <em>I was <strong>in</strong> the house</em> 🏠.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚡ Quiz</h2>\n<p>Scroll down! ⭐</p>"},{"id":"unit-11","label":"Unit 11: worked / got / went","title":"⚽ Unit 11: worked / got / went (Past Simple)","group":"The Past Tense","groupEmoji":"•","emoji":"⚽","blurb":"Past simple: regular -ed verbs and the famous irregulars.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Unit 11 — Past Simple","questions":[{"q":"Yesterday I ______ football with my brother.","options":["play","played","playing"],"answer":1,"why":"'Yesterday' + a finished action → past simple 'played'."},{"q":"The past of 'go' is…","options":["goed","went","gone to"],"answer":1,"why":"'Go' is irregular: go → went."},{"q":"She ______ a great film last night.","options":["see","saw","seed"],"answer":1,"why":"'See' is irregular: see → saw."},{"q":"What is the correct -ed spelling of 'stop'?","options":["stoped","stopped","stopd"],"answer":1,"why":"Short verb (consonant+vowel+consonant): double the last letter → stopped."},{"q":"What is the correct -ed spelling of 'study'?","options":["studyed","studied","studed"],"answer":1,"why":"Consonant + y → change y to ied: study → studied."},{"q":"Which time word means the past simple is needed?","options":["tomorrow","two days ago","every day"],"answer":1,"why":"'Ago', 'yesterday' and 'last…' all point to the finished past."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 5:00 → 10:00 (5 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Talk about <strong>finished actions</strong> in the past: <em>I played, she went, we saw.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Two kinds of past verbs</h2>\n<h3>1️⃣ Regular verbs — add <strong>-ed</strong> ✅ (easy!)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Now</th>\n<th>Yesterday</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>play →</td>\n<td><strong>played</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>watch →</td>\n<td><strong>watched</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>walk →</td>\n<td><strong>walked</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>finish →</td>\n<td><strong>finished</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>help →</td>\n<td><strong>helped</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clean →</td>\n<td><strong>cleaned</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>2️⃣ Irregular verbs — they change completely 🎲 (memorise these!)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Now</th>\n<th>Yesterday</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>go →</td>\n<td><strong>went</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get →</td>\n<td><strong>got</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>see →</td>\n<td><strong>saw</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>eat →</td>\n<td><strong>ate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>have →</td>\n<td><strong>had</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>make →</td>\n<td><strong>made</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>take →</td>\n<td><strong>took</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>come →</td>\n<td><strong>came</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>say →</td>\n<td><strong>said</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>do →</td>\n<td><strong>did</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give →</td>\n<td><strong>gave</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>buy →</td>\n<td><strong>bought</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>There's a <strong>full list</strong> on the 📖 Irregular Verbs page — print it and stick it on\nyour wall! 🧱</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/pastsimple.jpg\" alt=\"A girl kicking a football that has just left her foot, drawn with motion lines\">\n<figcaption>*\"I **kicked** the ball.\"* — one finished action ⚽</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The spelling rules for <strong>-ed</strong> (3 little rules)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Most verbs: just add <strong>-ed</strong></td>\n<td>walk → walk<strong>ed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End in <strong>e</strong>: add just <strong>-d</strong></td>\n<td>live → live<strong>d</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short verb ending <strong>consonant + vowel + consonant</strong>: <strong>double</strong> it</td>\n<td>stop → sto<strong>pp</strong>ed · plan → pla<strong>nn</strong>ed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End in <strong>consonant + y</strong>: change y → <strong>ied</strong></td>\n<td>study → stud<strong>ied</strong> · carry → carr<strong>ied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>(But: play → play<strong>ed</strong>, because a vowel comes before the y! 😉)</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 When do we use the past simple?</h2>\n<p>When the action is <strong>finished</strong> — often with these words:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time words</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>yesterday</strong></td>\n<td>⬅️ one day back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>last</strong> night / week / year</td>\n<td>⬅️</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>two days ago</strong></td>\n<td>⬅️</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>in 2020</strong></td>\n<td>⬅️</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>when I was little</strong></td>\n<td>⬅️</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I <strong>watched</strong> a film <strong>last night</strong>.</em> 🎬</li>\n<li><em>We <strong>went</strong> to the beach <strong>yesterday</strong>.</em> 🏖️</li>\n<li><em>She <strong>ate</strong> noodles <strong>two hours ago</strong>.</em> 🍜</li>\n</ul>\n<p>💡 If you see <strong>yesterday / last / ago</strong>, your verb is almost certainly a past\nsimple one!</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Telling a story in the past</h2>\n<p>Past simple is the <strong>story-telling tense</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Yesterday we <strong>went</strong> to the beach. I <strong>swam</strong> in the sea and my brother\n<strong>built</strong> a sandcastle. Then we <strong>ate</strong> ice cream and we <strong>went</strong> home.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Every verb is a <strong>finished step</strong> in the story. 🪜</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ The mistake to avoid</h2>\n<p>❌ <em>I <strong>go</strong> to the beach yesterday.</em> → ✅ <em>I <strong>went</strong> to the beach yesterday.</em></p>\n<p>❌ <em>She <strong>plays</strong> football last Saturday.</em> → ✅ <em>She <strong>played</strong> football last Saturday.</em></p>\n<p><strong>If the time word says past (<em>yesterday, last…, ago</em>), the verb must be past!</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚡ Quiz</h2>\n<p>Scroll down! ⭐</p>"},{"id":"unit-12","label":"Unit 12: didn't / Did you…? ⭐","title":"⭐ Unit 12: I didn't … Did you … ?","group":"The Past Tense","groupEmoji":"•","emoji":"⭐","blurb":"THE BIG ONE - negatives and questions, with the golden rule.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Unit 12 — didn't / Did you…? ⭐","questions":[{"q":"I ______ to school yesterday because I was ill.","options":["didn't went","didn't go","don't go"],"answer":1,"why":"'didn't' is already past → the next verb goes back to the base form: didn't GO. Never 'didn't went'!"},{"q":"______ you ______ the film last night?","options":["Did / saw","Did / see","Do / see"],"answer":1,"why":"Did + person + BASE verb: 'Did you see…?' Never 'Did you saw'."},{"q":"She didn't ______ her homework.","options":["finish","finished","finishing"],"answer":0,"why":"After 'didn't' the verb is always the base form: didn't FINISH."},{"q":"'Did you like the film?' — correct short answer?","options":["Yes, I liked.","Yes, I did.","Yes, I was."],"answer":1,"why":"Short answers use did/didn't: 'Yes, I did.'"},{"q":"Which question is CORRECT?","options":["Did you were tired?","Were you tired?","Did you was tired?"],"answer":1,"why":"There's no other verb here, only 'be' → use WERE at the front. did and was/were never go together."},{"q":"What is the negative of 'They ate the cake'?","options":["They didn't ate the cake.","They didn't eat the cake.","They not ate the cake."],"answer":1,"why":"ate → eat after didn't: 'They didn't eat the cake.'"},{"q":"Which sentence is wrong?","options":["I didn't see him.","Did she call you?","He didn't went home."],"answer":2,"why":"'didn't went' is wrong — it must be 'He didn't GO home.' Only one past is allowed!"},{"q":"Why must we say 'Did you go?' and not 'Did you went?'","options":["Because 'did' is already the past, so the main verb stays in base form","Because 'went' is not a real word","Because questions never use past verbs","Because 'did' is present"],"answer":0,"why":"One past only. 'Did' carries the past, so the main verb relaxes into its base form."}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 10:00 → 15:00 (5 minutes) — the big one!</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>The unit nearly everyone gets wrong — so this one gets <strong>extra time</strong>, <strong>its own\ncheat sheet</strong>, and the <strong>longest quiz</strong>. By the end you'll never write <em>\"Did you\nwent\"</em> again. 😎</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The golden rule (learn this one sentence)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>🏆 <strong>When you use <em>did</em> / <em>didn't</em>, the next verb goes BACK to normal.</strong></h2>\n<p><em>didn't <strong>go</strong></em> · <em>Did you <strong>go</strong>?</em> — <strong>never</strong> <em>didn't went</em>, <strong>never</strong> <em>Did you went</em>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why? Because <strong>did</strong> is already the past! Only <strong>one</strong> word needs to carry the\npast — so the main verb stays in its <strong>base form</strong> (the dictionary form).</p>\n<p>Think of it like this: 🏃‍♀️💨</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>did / didn't = the past-carrier.</strong> Once it's carrying the past, the other verb\ncan relax and wear its normal clothes. 👕</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 1. Saying NO: <strong>didn't + base verb</strong></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ What you did</th>\n<th>❌ What you didn't do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>went</strong> to school.</td>\n<td>I <strong>didn't go</strong> to school.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She <strong>played</strong> tennis.</td>\n<td>She <strong>didn't play</strong> tennis.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They <strong>ate</strong> the cake.</td>\n<td>They <strong>didn't eat</strong> the cake.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Notice: <strong>went → go</strong>, <strong>played → play</strong>, <strong>ate → eat</strong>.\n<strong>The past form disappears as soon as <em>didn't</em> arrives!</strong> ✨</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 2. Asking: <strong>Did + person + base verb … ?</strong></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Telling</th>\n<th>Asking</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>You <strong>went</strong> to school.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>go</strong> to school?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>saw</strong> the film.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Did</strong> she <strong>see</strong> the film?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They <strong>finished</strong> it.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Did</strong> they <strong>finish</strong> it?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/unit12.jpg\" alt=\"A girl shrugging with an empty thought bubble while a football lies still on the grass with a cross drawn over it\">\n<figcaption>*\"Did you kick the ball?\" — \"No, I **didn't**.\"* 🙈</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 3. Short answers (two parts, always!)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>✅ Yes</th>\n<th>❌ No</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Did you <strong>see</strong> him?</em></td>\n<td><em>Yes, I <strong>did</strong>.</em></td>\n<td><em>No, I <strong>didn't</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Did she <strong>like</strong> it?</em></td>\n<td><em>Yes, she <strong>did</strong>.</em></td>\n<td><em>No, she <strong>didn't</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>Never</strong> answer with just <em>\"Yes.\"</em> or <em>\"No.\"</em> — always finish with\n<strong>did / didn't</strong>. That's how you show you understood the past tense! ✅</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 4. The BIG question: <em>did</em> or <em>was/were</em>?</h2>\n<p>This catches everybody. Ask: <strong>is there a verb, or just a description?</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If the sentence has…</th>\n<th>Then the question is…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a normal verb (go, play, eat)</td>\n<td><strong>Did</strong> + person + verb? → <em>Did you <strong>go</strong>?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>no other verb</strong> — just be (was/were)</td>\n<td>swap <em>was/were</em> to the front → <em><strong>Were</strong> you tired?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>❌ Wrong</th>\n<th>✅ Right</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Did you <strong>were</strong> tired?</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Were</strong> you tired?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Did she <strong>was</strong> at home?</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Was</strong> she at home?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I didn't <strong>was</strong> tired.</em></td>\n<td><em>I <strong>wasn't</strong> tired.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>💡 <strong>was/were never meet <em>did</em>.</strong> They do their own job. 🚫</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 5. The three-sentence drill (do it out loud!)</h2>\n<p>For any verb, say all three forms:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>I went.</strong> → <strong>I didn't go.</strong> → <strong>Did you go?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Try it with: <em>play · eat · see · finish · have · make</em> 🗣️</p>\n<p><em>I played. I didn't play. Did you play?</em> ✅\n<em>I had lunch. I didn't have lunch. Did you have lunch?</em> ✅</p>\n<p><strong>Feel how the second and third sentences use the plain verb?</strong> That's the whole\nunit. You've got it. 🏆</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🎯 Unit 12 in three lines</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>didn't + base verb</strong> → <em>I didn't <strong>go</strong></em></li>\n<li><strong>Did + person + base verb</strong> → <em>Did you <strong>go</strong>?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Short answers need did/didn't</strong> → <em>Yes, I <strong>did</strong>. / No, I <strong>didn't</strong>.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check the ⭐ <strong>The <em>did</em> Rules</strong> page for the cheat sheet, then take the quiz —\nit's the longest one! 💪</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚡ Quiz (8 questions — the biggest!)</h2>\n<p>Scroll down! ⭐</p>"},{"id":"unit-13","label":"Unit 13: I was doing","title":"🏃 Unit 13: I was doing (Past Continuous)","group":"The Past Tense","groupEmoji":"•","emoji":"🏃","blurb":"Past continuous - the video that was playing.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Unit 13 — Past Continuous","questions":[{"q":"At 8 o'clock last night I ______ my homework.","options":["did","was doing","do"],"answer":1,"why":"An action in progress at a past moment → was/were + -ing."},{"q":"They ______ football when I arrived.","options":["was playing","were playing","played"],"answer":1,"why":"'They' → WERE + playing."},{"q":"What is the correct -ing form of 'run'?","options":["runing","running","runnning"],"answer":1,"why":"Short verb (consonant+vowel+consonant): double the last letter → running."},{"q":"What is the correct -ing form of 'write'?","options":["writeing","writing","writting"],"answer":1,"why":"Verbs ending in e drop the e: write → writing."},{"q":"'Was she sleeping?' — correct short answer?","options":["Yes, she was.","Yes, she did.","Yes, she slept."],"answer":0,"why":"Answer with the same helping word: 'Yes, she was.'"},{"q":"Which is correct?","options":["I was play football.","I was playing football.","I playing football."],"answer":1,"why":"was/were and -ing always go together — like shoes and socks!"}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 15:00 → 20:00 (5 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>Talk about an action that was <strong>in the middle of happening</strong> at a past time.</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/pastcontinuous.jpg\" alt=\"A girl running across a park, drawn mid-stride with motion blur and speed lines\">\n<figcaption>*\"At 3 o'clock I **was running**.\"* — the video is playing 🎬</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The rule</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<h2><strong>was / were + verb-ing</strong></h2>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Person</th>\n<th>Past continuous</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>I <strong>was playing</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>he / she / it</td>\n<td>She <strong>was reading</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you / we / they</td>\n<td>We <strong>were watching</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Same <em>was/were</em> rule as Unit 10</strong> — you already know that part! Then just add\n<strong>-ing</strong>. 🎉</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 When do we use it?</h2>\n<p>When an action was <strong>still going on</strong> at a moment in the past:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>At 8 o'clock last night I <strong>was doing</strong> my homework.</em> 📚</li>\n<li><em>This time yesterday we <strong>were flying</strong> to Japan.</em> ✈️</li>\n<li><em>When you called, I <strong>was having</strong> a shower.</em> 🚿</li>\n</ul>\n<p>💡 <strong>Photo vs video:</strong>\n- <em>I <strong>swam</strong>.</em> = one photo, finished 📸 (past simple)\n- <em>I <strong>was swimming</strong> at 3 o'clock.</em> = a video clip, still playing 🎬 (past continuous)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The spelling of <strong>-ing</strong> (3 rules)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Most verbs: add <strong>-ing</strong></td>\n<td>play → play<strong>ing</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verb ends in <strong>e</strong>: drop the e</td>\n<td>write → writ<strong>ing</strong> · make → mak<strong>ing</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short verb (consonant + vowel + consonant): <strong>double</strong> the last letter</td>\n<td>run → ru<strong>nn</strong>ing · sit → si<strong>tt</strong>ing · swim → swi<strong>mm</strong>ing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> <em>lie → lying</em>, <em>die → dying</em> (they're special! 🙂)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Saying NO and asking</h2>\n<p><strong>Negative:</strong> <em>wasn't / weren't + verb-ing</em>\n- <em>I <strong>wasn't</strong> sleeping. I was reading.</em> 👀\n- <em>They <strong>weren't</strong> listening.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> move <em>was/were</em> to the front (like Unit 10!)\n- <em><strong>Was</strong> she sleeping?</em>\n- <em><strong>Were</strong> you waiting for me?</em>\n- <em>Yes, I <strong>was</strong>. / No, I <strong>wasn't</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>⚠️ Notice: <strong>no <em>did</em> here either!</strong> <em>Was</em> and <em>were</em> do the job on their own.\n❌ <em>Did you were sleeping?</em> ✅ <em>Were you sleeping?</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The time words that love the past continuous</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>at 7 o'clock</strong></td>\n<td><em>At 7 o'clock I <strong>was eating</strong> breakfast.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>this time yesterday</strong></td>\n<td><em>This time yesterday I <strong>was sitting</strong> in class.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>while</strong></td>\n<td><em>While I <strong>was reading</strong>, he was drawing.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>all morning / all day</strong></td>\n<td><em>It <strong>was raining</strong> all morning.</em> ☔</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ The mistake to avoid</h2>\n<p>❌ <em>I <strong>was</strong> play football.</em> → ✅ <em>I <strong>was playing</strong> football.</em>\n<em>(was needs the -ing! They always go together. 👫)</em></p>\n<p>❌ <em>I <strong>playing</strong> football.</em> → ✅ <em>I <strong>was playing</strong> football.</em></p>\n<p><strong>was/were + doing</strong> — like shoes and socks. Always both! 🧦👟</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚡ Quiz</h2>\n<p>Scroll down! ⭐</p>"},{"id":"unit-14","label":"Unit 14: was doing + did","title":"🎬 Unit 14: I was doing **and** I did","group":"The Past Tense","groupEmoji":"•","emoji":"🎬","blurb":"Putting both past tenses together - while and when.","kind":"doc","quiz":{"title":"Unit 14 — was doing + did","questions":[{"q":"I ______ home when it started to rain.","options":["walked","was walking","walk"],"answer":1,"why":"The long background action = was walking. 'Started' is the short interruption."},{"q":"We were watching TV when the lights ______ out.","options":["go","were going","went"],"answer":2,"why":"The short 'pop!' interruption takes the past simple: went."},{"q":"______ I was having a bath, the phone rang.","options":["While","When","During"],"answer":0,"why":"'While' goes with the long action (was having). 'When' fits the short one."},{"q":"Which sentence is correct?","options":["While I walked home, it was starting to rain.","While I was walking home, it started to rain.","While I was walking home, it was starting to rain."],"answer":1,"why":"Long action → was walking. Short 'pop!' action → started."},{"q":"They ______ talking while we ______ eating. (two long actions together)","options":["were / were","was / was","were / was"],"answer":0,"why":"Two long actions at the same time → past continuous for both. 'They' and 'we' both take were."},{"q":"Both actions are long. What do you use?","options":["past simple for both","was/were + -ing for both","one of each"],"answer":1,"why":"Two long actions running at once → two past continuous verbs: 'While I was reading, he was drawing.'"}]},"html":"<p><strong>⏱️ 20:00 → 25:00 (5 minutes)</strong></p>\n<h2>🎯 Goal</h2>\n<p>The cleverest unit in the whole past section: putting <strong>both past tenses together</strong>\nin one sentence.</p>\n<div class=\"pic-frame\">\n<figure>\n<img src=\"assets/unit14.jpg\" alt=\"A girl walking with an umbrella in the rain while a splash from a passing puddle startles her\">\n<figcaption>*\"I **was walking** home when a car **splashed** me!\"* 💦</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The big idea: long action + short action</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tense</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Picture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>was doing</strong> (past continuous)</td>\n<td>the <strong>long</strong> background action</td>\n<td>🎥 the video playing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>did</strong> (past simple)</td>\n<td>the <strong>short</strong> action that interrupts it</td>\n<td>⚡ the photo <em>BANG!</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<h2><em>I <strong>was walking</strong> home when it <strong>started</strong> to rain.</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>was walking</em> = the long action (going on for minutes) 🚶‍♀️</li>\n<li><em>started</em> = the short action that interrupted it ⚡</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The two magic words</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Goes with</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>while</strong></td>\n<td>the <strong>long</strong> action → <strong>was doing</strong></td>\n<td><em><strong>While</strong> I <strong>was having</strong> a bath, the phone rang.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>when</strong></td>\n<td>the <strong>short</strong> action → <strong>did</strong></td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was having</strong> a bath <strong>when</strong> the phone <strong>rang</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>💡 Both sentences describe the <strong>same moment</strong> — just with the focus in a different\nplace! 🤯</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 More examples (say them out loud 🗣️)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>We <strong>were watching</strong> TV <strong>when</strong> the lights <strong>went</strong> out.</em> 💡</li>\n<li><em><strong>While</strong> she <strong>was cooking</strong>, she <strong>dropped</strong> the spoon.</em> 🥄</li>\n<li><em>They <strong>were playing</strong> football <strong>when</strong> it <strong>began</strong> to rain.</em> ⚽🌧️</li>\n<li><em>I <strong>was crossing</strong> the road <strong>when</strong> I <strong>saw</strong> my teacher.</em> 👀</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice: the <strong>short</strong> action is always the interesting one — that's the\n<strong>interruption</strong>! 🎯</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Which one do I choose? (the 3-second test)</h2>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>\"Was it going on for a while, or did it just happen <em>pop</em>?\"</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Going on for a while 🔄</th>\n<th>Just happened, pop! ⚡</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>was/were + <strong>doing</strong></td>\n<td>past simple (<strong>did / went / saw</strong>…)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>was sleeping, were eating, was raining</em></td>\n<td><em>rang, fell, started, saw, broke</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 Bonus: two long actions at once</h2>\n<p>If <strong>both</strong> actions are long, use <strong>was doing</strong> for both:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>While</strong> I <strong>was doing</strong> my homework, my brother <strong>was playing</strong> the piano.</em></li>\n<li><em>They <strong>were talking</strong> <strong>while</strong> we <strong>were eating</strong>.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>(Two long actions = two past continuous verbs. No past simple needed!)</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ The mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<p>❌ <em>While I <strong>walked</strong> home, it <strong>was starting</strong> to rain.</em> ← backwards! 🔄\n✅ <em>While I <strong>was walking</strong> home, it <strong>started</strong> to rain.</em> ✅</p>\n<p><strong>The LONG action gets <em>was doing</em>. The SHORT interruption gets the plain past.</strong></p>\n<p>❌ <em>When it <strong>was starting</strong> to rain, I was walking home.</em>\n✅ <em>When it <strong>started</strong> to rain, I was walking home.</em> ✅</p>\n<p><em>Start</em> is a <strong>pop!</strong> action — so it takes past simple. ⚡</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 You now know all five!</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>You can say…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was</strong> at the beach.</em> 🏖️</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>played</strong>, I <strong>went</strong>.</em> ⚽</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>didn't go</strong>. <strong>Did you go</strong>?</em> ⭐</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was playing</strong> at 4 o'clock.</em> 🎬</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>was playing</strong> when it <strong>started</strong> to rain.</em> ⚡</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That's the <strong>whole past tense</strong>. Congratulations, Abigail! 🏆</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚡ Final quiz</h2>\n<p>Scroll down! ⭐</p>"},{"id":"did-rules","label":"The did Rules ⭐","title":"⭐ The *did* Rules (Unit 12 Cheat Sheet)","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"📋","blurb":"The Unit 12 cheat sheet - print it.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p>Print this page. It fixes the most common mistake in the whole past tense. 🖨️</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🏆 The golden rule</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>One past only!</strong>\n<em>did / didn't</em> is <strong>already past</strong> — so the next verb goes back to its\n<strong>base form</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>❌ Wrong</th>\n<th>✅ Right</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I didn't <strong>went</strong></td>\n<td>I didn't <strong>go</strong></td>\n<td><em>didn't</em> = past already</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you <strong>saw</strong> it?</td>\n<td>Did you <strong>see</strong> it?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> = past already</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She didn't <strong>ate</strong></td>\n<td>She didn't <strong>eat</strong></td>\n<td>same rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did they <strong>finished</strong>?</td>\n<td>Did they <strong>finish</strong>?</td>\n<td>same rule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>📋 The three forms of any verb</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Form</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>➕ Telling</td>\n<td>I <strong>went</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>➖ Saying no</td>\n<td>I <strong>didn't go</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❓ Asking</td>\n<td><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>go</strong>?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Practise with <em>eat, see, play, have, take, write, make, buy</em>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>I <strong>had</strong> breakfast. → I <strong>didn't have</strong> breakfast. → <strong>Did</strong> you <strong>have</strong> breakfast?</em> ✅</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>🗣️ Short answers (always two parts!)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>✅</th>\n<th>❌</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you like it?</td>\n<td>Yes, I <strong>did</strong>.</td>\n<td>No, I <strong>didn't</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did she call?</td>\n<td>Yes, she <strong>did</strong>.</td>\n<td>No, she <strong>didn't</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>🚫 <em>did</em> and <em>was/were</em> never work together</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Correct question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>There's a <strong>verb</strong> (go, play, eat)</td>\n<td><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>go</strong>?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>There's <strong>only be</strong> (was/were)</td>\n<td><strong>Were</strong> you tired?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>❌ <em>Did you were tired?</em> ❌ <em>Did she was home?</em> ❌ <em>I didn't was happy.</em></p>\n<p>✅ <em>Were you tired?</em> ✅ <em>Was she home?</em> ✅ <em>I wasn't happy.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🔤 Word order (the sentence skeleton)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Did + person + base verb + the rest + ?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Did</td>\n<td><strong>Did</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>person</td>\n<td><strong>you</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>base verb</td>\n<td><strong>finish</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rest</td>\n<td>your homework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>→ <strong><em>Did you finish your homework?</em></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Negative skeleton:</strong> <strong>person + didn't + base verb</strong>\n→ <em>She <strong>didn't finish</strong> her homework.</em> ✅</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧪 30-second self-test</h2>\n<p>Say all three out loud for <em>\"see a film\"</em> 🎬:</p>\n<p><em>I <strong>saw</strong> a film. → I <strong>didn't see</strong> a film. → <strong>Did</strong> you <strong>see</strong> a film?</em></p>\n<p>If the middle and last ones used <strong>see</strong> (not <em>saw</em>) — you've mastered Unit 12. 🏆</p>"},{"id":"irregulars","label":"Irregular Verbs","title":"📖 Irregular Verbs (Yesterday Forms)","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"📖","blurb":"The yesterday-forms, in rhyming families.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p>Print this. Regular verbs add <strong>-ed</strong>; these famous ones change completely. Learn\n<strong>five a day</strong> and you'll know them all in two weeks. 🧱</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>⭐ The 12 you'll use every day</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Today</th>\n<th>Yesterday</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>be (am/is/are)</td>\n<td><strong>was / were</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>go</td>\n<td><strong>went</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>get</td>\n<td><strong>got</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>have</td>\n<td><strong>had</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>do</td>\n<td><strong>did</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>say</td>\n<td><strong>said</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>see</td>\n<td><strong>saw</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>come</td>\n<td><strong>came</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>make</td>\n<td><strong>made</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>take</td>\n<td><strong>took</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>eat</td>\n<td><strong>ate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give</td>\n<td><strong>gave</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>➕ Twelve more</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Today</th>\n<th>Yesterday</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>buy</td>\n<td><strong>bought</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bring</td>\n<td><strong>brought</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>think</td>\n<td><strong>thought</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>find</td>\n<td><strong>found</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tell</td>\n<td><strong>told</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>read</td>\n<td><strong>read</strong> <em>(say \"red\")</em> 🔴</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>write</td>\n<td><strong>wrote</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>run</td>\n<td><strong>ran</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>swim</td>\n<td><strong>swam</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sing</td>\n<td><strong>sang</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>drink</td>\n<td><strong>drank</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sleep</td>\n<td><strong>slept</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>➕ And these</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Today</th>\n<th>Yesterday</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>know</td>\n<td><strong>knew</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>leave</td>\n<td><strong>left</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feel</td>\n<td><strong>felt</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>meet</td>\n<td><strong>met</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>pay</td>\n<td><strong>paid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>put</td>\n<td><strong>put</strong> <em>(same!)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cut</td>\n<td><strong>cut</strong> <em>(same!)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sit</td>\n<td><strong>sat</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stand</td>\n<td><strong>stood</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>understand</td>\n<td><strong>understood</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>begin</td>\n<td><strong>began</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>break</td>\n<td><strong>broke</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fly</td>\n<td><strong>flew</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>forget</td>\n<td><strong>forgot</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hear</td>\n<td><strong>heard</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lose</td>\n<td><strong>lost</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>speak</td>\n<td><strong>spoke</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>win</td>\n<td><strong>won</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>🎵 The rhyming families (easier to remember!)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Verbs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>-ought / -aught</strong></td>\n<td>buy → bought · bring → brought · think → thought · teach → taught</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>-ew</strong></td>\n<td>fly → flew · know → knew · grow → grew · throw → threw</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>-old / -old</strong></td>\n<td>tell → told · sell → sold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>-ang / -ung</strong></td>\n<td>sing → sang · ring → rang · swim → swam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>same word</strong></td>\n<td>put · cut · hit · let · shut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>⚠️ Remember: after <em>did / didn't</em>, use the <strong>TODAY</strong> form!</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Did you <strong>go</strong>?</em> ✅ (not <em>went</em>)</li>\n<li><em>I didn't <strong>eat</strong> it.</em> ✅ (not <em>ate</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The \"yesterday\" form only appears when it is the ONLY past word in the\nsentence.</strong> 🏆</p>"},{"id":"grownups","label":"For Parents & Teachers","title":"🧑‍🏫 For Parents & Teachers","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"🧑‍🏫","blurb":"What each unit teaches and the three errors to watch for.","kind":"doc","quiz":null,"html":"<p>A short note about this course. Three minutes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🎯 What it covers</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Past</strong> section, <strong>Units 10–14</strong>, of Abigail's grammar course:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>Target language</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><em>was / were</em> (past of <em>be</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>past simple — regular <em>-ed</em> + irregulars (<em>worked / got / went</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><strong>past simple negatives &amp; questions</strong> — <em>I didn't … Did you …?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>past continuous — <em>I was doing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>past continuous + past simple together — <em>I was doing … when I did …</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Unit 12 receives deliberate extra treatment</strong> (its own step, its own cheat-sheet\npage, the longest quiz) because it is where most learners lose marks — usually by\ncarrying the past into the verb after <em>did</em>: ❌ <em>Did you went</em>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🧠 The three errors this course is designed to prevent</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>\"Did you went?\"</strong> → the main verb after <em>did/didn't</em> must be the <strong>base form</strong></li>\n<li><strong>\"Did you were tired?\"</strong> → <em>was/were</em> never combine with <em>did</em> (→ <em>Were you tired?</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>\"I was play football.\"</strong> → past continuous always needs <strong>was/were + -ing</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each is explicitly named and drilled in the course, then tested in the quizzes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>🗣️ How to use it at home</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>One unit per sitting</strong> (5 minutes each) — or all five in one 25-minute run.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>on-page timer</strong> so she can see the pace.</li>\n<li>Do the <strong>three-sentence drill</strong> out loud for any verb: <em>\"I went. I didn't go. Did\n   you go?\"</em> — this is the single highest-value 30 seconds of practice.</li>\n<li>Print <strong>⭐ The <em>did</em> Rules</strong> and <strong>📖 Irregular Verbs</strong> and put them where she\n   does homework.</li>\n<li>When she gets a quiz question wrong, the feedback explains <strong>why</strong> — read it\n   together rather than just re-answering.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>📏 Realistic expectations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>was/were</em> and regular <em>-ed</em> land quickly (usually within a week).</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular verbs take real repetition</strong> — 5 per day, spoken aloud, beats a long\n  list copied out once.</li>\n<li>Unit 12's rule is <em>understood</em> immediately but needs <strong>spaced practice</strong> to\n  become automatic; re-run that step's quiz a few days later.</li>\n<li>Unit 14 (the two tenses together) is the hardest — expect it to need a second\n  pass, and be reassured that it's normally introduced exactly like this, after the\n  two tenses are solid separately.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><em>25 minutes, quiz-only, no writing required. Runs in the browser; nothing to\ninstall; progress saved on the device.</em></p>"}]};
